{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>The kindred of the house, or the kindred from without.</b> Whether they say to your father, \"she is valid,\" or whether they say to your father, \"put her out\" — for example, a mamzeret or a netinah — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 18:9). And it is not correct, for it would follow from this that his sister from among the forbidden relations would not be included in this prohibition, yet they are all subject to karet, except for one born of a maidservant or a gentile woman. And I am astonished at the Rav, for behold, in the Gemara in tractate Yevamot in the second chapter (23a) it is explicit: \"And say it excludes his sister from among those liable for negative commandments.\" Rav Pappa said: \"Those liable for negative commandments — kiddushin take effect with them, as it is written (Deuteronomy 21:15), 'If a man have two wives, the one beloved and the one hated' — but is there one beloved before the Place and one hated before the Place? Rather, 'beloved' — beloved in her marriage; 'hated' — hated in her marriage, and the Merciful One said, 'if they be to one man.'\" \"And say it excludes his sister from among those liable for karet.\" Rava said: \"The verse says (here), 'The nakedness of your sister, the daughter of your father or the daughter of your mother, whether born at home or born abroad' — whether they say to your father, 'she is valid,' or whether they say to your father, 'put her out,' and the Merciful One said, 'she is your sister.'\" Now, the meaning of \"they say to him, 'put her out'\" is that you must put her out of your house — she is not your wife and kiddushin do not take effect with her, for they are subject to karet and to death by the court, and even so he is liable on account of \"his sister.\" But his sister from a maidservant or from a gentile woman is not included in this prohibition, because the offspring is like her. \\n\\n And according to the plain sense, one would say: \"The nakedness of your sister, the daughter of your father or the daughter of your mother\" — born of marriage, which the man brought into his house, or \"born abroad,\" where a man found her outside, took her and lay with her, and she bore him a child — \"you shall not uncover her nakedness.\" In the case of a maternal sister, Scripture mentioned both one born of marriage and one born of harlotry, for even though each of them is a daughter of his mother and not a daughter of his father, one might suppose that it is fitting to conduct the honor of sisterhood toward one born of marriage but not toward one who comes from harlotry, for all is unrestricted. Therefore Scripture needed to warn concerning all. \\n\\n And furthermore, regarding his paternal sister, another verse is written (Leviticus 18:11): \"The nakedness of your father's wife's daughter, begotten of your father\" — and she is his paternal sister born of marriage. And it was necessary to mention this because Scripture had mentioned \"the nakedness of your sister, the daughter of your father\" without specification, and it specified regarding a maternal sister \"born at home\" — in marriage — \"or born abroad\" — in harlotry, and one might reason from this that a paternal daughter from harlotry would be permitted, for perhaps the Torah would not take heed of the kinship of a man that comes from harlotry, because the offspring are not known to him. Therefore it explained and said: \"The nakedness of your father's wife's daughter, begotten of your father,\" to declare that the daughter of his father mentioned at the outset is not the daughter of his father's wife. And because among the descendants of Noah there was a prohibition regarding sisterhood from the mother but not from the father (Sanhedrin 58a), Scripture explains that in Israel they are all equal in the prohibition. \\n\\n And the correct interpretation in the plain sense of this verse is what the Sages said in the Gemara in tractate Yevamot (Yevamot 22a): one who lies with his sister who is also the daughter of his father's wife but not his mother is liable on account of \"his sister\" and on account of \"his father's wife's daughter.\" And it explains there: \"Since it is written here, 'The nakedness of your sister, the daughter of your father,' why do I need 'the daughter of your father's wife, begotten of your father'? Learn from it that they are liable on account of 'his sister' and on account of 'his father's wife's daughter.'\" And Scripture repeats the negative commandments to make one liable for each and every designation, for even concerning creeping things Scripture repeats many negative commandments under a single name to receive lashes for them."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "Ramban cites two Talmudic passages by chapter number; the page references in parentheses (23a, 22a, 58a) appear to be editorial additions in the printed edition, not Ramban's own."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:55:39.215654755Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}